WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.963
- Welcome everybody, thank you for coming.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:15.690
The professors with me on stage

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.690
are brilliant scholars in their fields

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.820
who also have a full life on Twitter,

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.520
and we're here to talk
about what that means

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.460
for their intellectual, social,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.180
professional, and personal lives.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.778
This introduction was
280 characters (laughs).

00:00:29.778 --> 00:00:32.990
(audience laughs)

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.380
So there are a lot of conversations

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.563
about what academics are doing on Twitter.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.300
This is a fairly good representation

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.120
of some of the marked media abuses,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.490
from procrastinating, gossiping
about people in one's field,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.740
having interactions with people

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.300
they have an intellectual crush on,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.640
but because Twitter's been around

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.580
for a while now and academics
have been on it for a while,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.100
there've been a lot of reactions

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.030
in the press about, and
in the scholarly press,

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.863
about whether this is good or bad.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.360
What are the purposes, is
it, the scholar for instance

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.300
of that New York Times
op-ed, the one that went,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.950
Quit Social Media, Your
Career May Depend on It,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.790
said, he's a young Georgetown professor

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.257
named Cal Newport, he wrote, "The idea

00:01:27.257 --> 00:01:29.357
"of purposefully introducing into my life

00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:32.177
"a service designed to
fragment my attention

00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:33.787
"is as scary to me as the idea

00:01:33.787 --> 00:01:36.917
"of smoking would be to
an endurance athlete,

00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:39.557
"and it should be to you if you're serious

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:41.647
"about creating things that matter."

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.950
Well, we have some differing opinions

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.330
among our distinguished panelists.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.580
Teresa Chahine is doing the work

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.270
that so many of us talked about.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.930
She is the--

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.063
And Teresa is in green here.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.220
She is the inaugural Sheila and Ron

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.890
Marcelo Lecturer in
social entrepreneurship,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.120
the author of Introduction
to Social Entrepreneurship,

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.020
a 12-step framework for building

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.290
impactful ventures in new
and existing organizations.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.180
Her research focuses on developing tools

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.810
to characterize and advance social

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.920
and environmental determinants of health.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.810
She also launched the first
social entrepreneurship

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.370
program in the context of
public health at Harvard.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.730
She was responsible for launching

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.750
the first venture
philanthropy organization

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.660
in her home country of Lebanon,

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.550
providing tailored financial and critical

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.080
management support to social enterprises,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.630
serving marginalized populations

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.593
through education and job
creation for youth and women.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.740
If Alexander Hamilton had Twitter,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.149
then he would be
retweeting Joanne Freeman.

00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:47.100
(audience laughs)

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.480
She is professor of history
and American studies

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:50.730
and is a leading expert

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.410
on early American politics and culture,

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.480
the author of the
award-winning Affairs of Honor:

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.300
National Politics in the New Republic,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.950
and editor of Alexander Hamilton: Writings

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.160
and The Essential Hamilton.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.000
Her work informed the writing
of Broadway's Hamilton.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.580
A co-host of the popular
American history podcast

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.120
Backstory, which I
recommend everyone download

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.406
immediately after exiting this conference,

00:03:12.406 --> 00:03:13.239
(panelists laugh)

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:15.120
and a commenter on documentaries

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.190
on PBS The History Channel, she also has

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.890
an active interest in
public-minded history.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.950
Her most recent book The Field of Blood:

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.100
Violence in Congress and
the Road to Civil War

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.710
was a New York Times notable book for 2018

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.803
and a finalist for the Lincoln Prize.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:34.620
Michael Kraus, there,
holding a coffee cup,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.320
is an exceptionally prolific scholar

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.510
and is also an exceptionally
prolific tweeter.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.750
He is an assistant professor
at the School of Management,

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.790
trained as a social psychology,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.440
social personality
psychologist at Berkeley,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.780
where he received his B.A.
in psychology and sociology

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.010
and PhD in social psychology.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.820
Before arriving at Yale, he was a postdoc

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.160
at UC San Francisco and then
at University of Illinois.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.380
His research can be found in
leading psychology journals

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.350
and has been featured in media outlets

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.210
like the New York Times,
New York Magazine,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.650
The Wall Street Journal,
NPR, ABC World News.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.220
Michael is active on Twitter
as you will soon learn

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.900
where he shares research news and comments

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.193
on politics and social inequality.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:22.440
Fiona Scott Morton, next to
me, has been justly called

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.970
a rockstar for portraying
disciplined economic literacy

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.690
and concern for enforcement
in the consumer interest.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.912
She was recently named a
radical by The New Republic.

00:04:31.912 --> 00:04:36.912
(audience applauds)
(panelists laugh)

00:04:36.942 --> 00:04:37.892
- Undeserved.

00:04:37.892 --> 00:04:40.480
- She teaches economics at
the School of Management

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.370
where she's been on
the faculty since 1999.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.670
Her area of research is
empirical industrial organization

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.540
with a focus on empirical studies

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.680
of competition in areas like pricing,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.720
entry, and product differentiation.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.200
Her public articles range widely

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.700
across industries from magazines

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.930
to shipping to pharmaceuticals
to internet retailing

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.100
and are published in
leading economic journals.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.670
From 2011 to 2012, Professor Scott Morton

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.540
served as Deputy
Assistant Attorney General

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.630
for Economics in the Antitrust Division

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.510
of the U.S. Department of Justice,

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.580
where she helped enforce
the nation's antitrust laws.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.560
Yale SOM, she teaches courses in the area

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.583
of competitive strategy
and many other honors.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.760
And you can see a little bit of her

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.870
most recent Tweets here.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:33.530
And finally, Jason Stanley
knows the world is on fire,

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.550
but he can laugh about it.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.890
He's the Jacob Urowsky
Professor of Philosophy

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.790
at Yale University, the
author of five books,

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.400
most recently How Fascism Works,

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.340
the winner of the 2007 Academic
Philosophical Association

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.270
Book Prize for his 2005 book,

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.240
Knowledge and Practical Interests.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.370
He writes on propaganda, authoritarianism,

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.090
mass incarceration, free
speech, and other issues

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.120
for the Times, Boston
Review, Washington Post,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.400
Chronicle of Higher Ed,
and other publications.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.113
He serves on the Board of
Prison Policy Initiative.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.510
Now before we get into
our serious discussion

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.117
of Twitter and all that it can do

00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:12.960
for professors in their serious lives,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.443
we know what, how things
can end up often going.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:22.670
So I'd like to start by
asking each of the panelists

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:27.670
how they started on Twitter
and how they use it now.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:28.983
Fiona?

00:06:28.983 --> 00:06:29.853
- Okay, sure.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:35.364
I joined Twitter because Steve
Berry guilted me into it.

00:06:35.364 --> 00:06:40.140
He said people were asking
where are all the IO economists

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.700
on Twitter, there don't seem to be any,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.880
and that was true, there really weren't.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.500
So I joined, and I use it really entirely

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.320
as a work platform, I don't
do anything social in Twitter.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.073
Well occasionally, someone
in my group will say,

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.460
you know, I got a journal article

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.980
or here's the bar cart, we're
having a happy hour, whatever.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.010
But that's pretty infrequent.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:02.483
Mostly I use it as a work platform,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:06.840
both to comment on the
economics of policy,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.670
mostly antitrust enforcement or healthcare

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.430
kind of policy choices
made by the government.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.700
And then also, I feel that it's a way

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.310
to keep in touch with former students

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.350
who were interested in
the material in my class

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.460
and which is related to current events,

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.050
and I can keep telling them about how

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.600
that class material
relates to current events.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.020
So that's really how I use it,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.963
and I find it to be
really great in that way.

00:07:34.065 --> 00:07:38.250
I think I, because of
using it as a work vehicle,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.080
I don't have much of the
snarky piling on stuff

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.570
that seems to happen in the rest Twitter

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.010
and I only follow about 50 people,

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.510
most of whom don't tweet very much,

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.840
so that I'm getting just the events

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.253
in my field coming into my feed.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.680
- [Emily] Thank you, Joanne?

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.099
- I am having a very different
Twitter experience from you.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:07:59.730
(both laugh)

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:04.730
I first got on Twitter
pretty much against my will.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:09.683
I'm low tech, slow tech, and
I wasn't even on Facebook.

00:08:09.683 --> 00:08:11.330
And I did, I don't know if any of you

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:12.930
have heard of the OpED Project,

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.320
but it's a great a project
that's getting women

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.190
and people of color out into the world,

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:20.340
being public commentators
and writing op-eds,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.500
so I did that here at
Yale, and they insisted

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.160
that everyone who's in the program

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.680
have a social media
presence, and in the session

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:28.830
that we were in, in the class,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.830
they said, right now, you're
all gonna sign up for Twitter

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:32.663
while you're sitting right here

00:08:32.663 --> 00:08:35.640
and you're gonna tweet
something, which I did,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.913
and then basically didn't
really know what to do with it.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:42.880
I got a sense of what it could do

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:46.423
during the last presidential election.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.100
I think before that, I
was probably tweeting out

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.280
occasional interesting
historical things I found,

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.210
new databases that I thought
colleagues would like,

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.100
interesting pieces of historical
evidence that I found,

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.690
but during the election I guess there was

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:06.400
a particularly strident
attack against the judiciary,

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.660
and I went on Twitter
and I even apologized,

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:12.030
I said, I know we all know this,

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.230
but I feel the need to talk
about checks and balances,

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:18.060
the balance of power, and
why the separation of powers

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.530
is important in this country,
so that you understand

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.740
why attacking the
judiciary is a bad thing.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.770
And I didn't, I guess I sort of assumed,

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.730
I apologized 'cause I thought
a lot of my colleagues

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:31.383
would be looking at it, but
I was going insane with,

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:34.190
anyway, I sent that out there,

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:38.430
and I got this stream of,
huge response from the public,

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.740
saying, thank you for
explaining that to us.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.260
And it dawned on me, number one,

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.207
people didn't necessarily know that,

00:09:47.207 --> 00:09:51.550
and number two, what an
incredible teaching tool.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:53.530
Right, in that moment,

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.210
that something important was communicated,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.720
who knows how many people.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.280
But so, I use it for a
variety of different ways,

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.610
I keep in touch with colleagues,

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:04.990
I stay on top of news and
also things about my field,

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:08.450
but I feel really aggressively

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.980
that I speak to the public about history,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:14.410
about the government, I
use a lot of documents

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.590
so I'm not screaming,
actually very deliberately

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:20.350
I'm not screaming, I'm very low key,

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.700
and I try to not allow
emotion to be on there at all.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.780
This morning I woke up and I said,

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.660
good morning, I'm gonna send out

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.280
the Presidential Oath of
Office for people to see,

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.380
here you go,

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.600
and I didn't say anything
more than that really.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.807
I then connected things to
it and said more than that,

00:10:35.807 --> 00:10:36.853
but at any rate.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:40.860
It's an incredible teaching
tool, that's my main takeaway.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.040
- [Emily] I think you told
me your second follower

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.070
or your first follower
was Lin Manuel Miranda.

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.950
- My second, yeah, so yeah.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.160
So the one thing I did on Twitter

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:54.090
was I knew two people on
Twitter, I knew my friend

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.560
and my friend who had told
me that he was on Twitter.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.670
And so when I joined it
and made my one tweet

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.260
and then I went to find my friend,

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.700
and then I thought the
only other person I know

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.220
who's on here is Lin Manuel Miranda,

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.330
and he happened to make
an Alexander Hamilton joke

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:10.220
right when I beamed in, and I responded.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.601
And he was like, Joanne Freeman?

00:11:12.601 --> 00:11:13.710
(audience laughs)
And then suddenly I was like

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.840
whoa, there are these people,
there's a conversation,

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.114
so yeah.

00:11:17.114 --> 00:11:18.210
- [Emily] Very cool.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:19.100
Teresa.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.420
- So I first joined
Twitter as a post-doctoral

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.070
research fellow when I was just graduated

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:27.100
from public health and I wanted to connect

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.430
with the social
entrepreneurship community.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.690
I was in a university that's very siloed

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.330
in terms of discipline and
the different graduate schools

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:36.720
don't really talk to each other,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.220
and social entrepreneurship
isn't really, or at least

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.630
at the time wasn't really
an academic discipline,

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:44.800
it's people who have studied public policy

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.180
or public health or management
and they're all working

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.570
in this innovative, cross-cutting area.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.410
And so that's what I was
using Twitter for mostly,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.420
to find out who the key stakeholders are,

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.860
what different people were doing,

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.490
and to just get involved
in the conversation.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:02.650
So I started following institutions

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.570
that support social entrepreneurs

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.580
and people out there doing what I thought

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.480
were amazing, daring, bold things,

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.580
and just learning about what's going on

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.600
and how are people doing
things differently,

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:17.870
'cause I had a very
mainstream academic upbringing

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:20.660
and I just wanted to
kind of break the silos.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:21.960
So that's really what it means to me,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.170
it's about breaking silos,

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:27.290
extending beyond your academic
discipline, and creating,

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.050
if not new disciplines then
just new ways of doing things,

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:32.660
talking to the unusual suspects

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.050
and hearing different voices and opinions.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:37.800
And I also use it a lot
to bridge my two worlds,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.650
because I come from Lebanon
and I spend a lot of time there

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:42.690
and my grassroots work is there.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.130
For a while, I was running the Twitter

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.420
and all the social media
of my organization,

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.020
Alfanar, which means Lighthouse in Arabic.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:51.850
It's a venture philanthropy organization.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:55.520
So I learned about comms
and social media at the time

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.630
'cause I had to become
savvy about what works

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:00.640
and what doesn't and when do
people see their Twitter feed

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:02.350
and how you should always have a photo

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:05.610
and those types of things
that I had no idea about,

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.520
'cause I'm trained as a
number cruncher basically.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.740
And so, so yeah, I learned to enjoy it,

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.470
and then when I created a textbook

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:17.700
based on my course on
social entrepreneurship,

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.290
I realized it's a good way to share it

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.490
and hear what people think
and that's when I learned

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.750
that it's a great way to
build a new community,

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:27.140
because I started getting tweets

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:29.920
from people who would say
oh, I'm on the second page

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.780
of this book and I know
I'll use it in my course,

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:33.920
and I was like, wow, that's incredible.

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.630
And then I reached out to that person

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:37.760
and we Skyped and we became friends

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.240
and we met up at a conference
and then I helped her

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.610
get into a fellowship and she
helped me think through things

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.560
and so now she's one of my closest friends

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.273
and colleagues just from a tweet.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.850
So those kinds of of things, I feel that,

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.340
you know, there can be magic
that happens on Twitter.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:56.320
So I'm not a frequent user,
I mostly just create lists,

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:58.510
like when I came to Yale,
I created a Yale list

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.590
that I try to check as often as I can.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:03.990
I think it's a great way
to connect with people.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:06.383
- [Emily] Michael.

00:14:06.383 --> 00:14:10.503
- So I guess I've been on
Twitter for more than 10 years,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.440
and then I was a graduate
student when I started,

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.290
so you can imagine if you think
you have something to say,

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.270
there's not necessarily
people who are interested

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.280
in what you're saying at that time.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:24.790
So I think early on,
Twitter was more of a way

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:28.610
to curate news for me,
so I was reading it,

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:31.490
understanding it, if there,
there weren't a lot of people

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:35.850
in anybody's field on Twitter early on,

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:39.110
so the platform kind of grew
into a place where you could,

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.570
like what some of the other
people here are saying,

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.170
like connect to people in your field,

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:47.760
be updated on current events,
be updated on current studies

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.750
in your field, and then sort of,

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:54.090
you know, over the course of your career,

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:55.800
people start listening to you,

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.890
and so my use of the platform
sort of starts to change

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:03.890
to be more of what, what am
I gonna say in this space?

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.840
There's a lot of discourse,
how can you use your science

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:09.420
and your research and your scholarship

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:10.950
to inform that discourse?

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:15.810
So it's taken on more of
that, although it's still,

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:19.152
for me at least, dad jokes as well.

00:15:19.152 --> 00:15:21.110
(audience laughs)
Yeah.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:21.943
- [Emily] Jason.

00:15:21.943 --> 00:15:24.750
- So I'm one of the few
humanists on Twitter

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:25.750
who's not a historian.

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:28.280
So historians are sort
of making their mark

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.763
with Joanne and Kevin Krauss and others.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:35.300
I avoided Twitter, I was a Facebook person

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.090
and I had this Facebook account

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:39.850
that had all the editors
of the major newspapers

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:42.980
and magazines on it, and
that's where I did my work

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:45.030
and shared pictures of my kids.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:49.907
And then I had a Facebook post that,

00:15:49.907 --> 00:15:52.030
it was a private Facebook post,

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:53.387
but the right wing somehow screenshot it

00:15:53.387 --> 00:15:56.440
and there was like 20 articles
in the right wing press

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.900
and I got all this hate
stuff and I started realizing

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:01.490
I probably shouldn't have a page

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.310
that has pictures of my family.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:05.630
And so I deleted my Facebook account,

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:09.070
this was in fall 2016, and then,

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:11.520
and I wanted a platform
that it would force me

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:15.750
never to do anything
personal (laughs) on it,

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:17.910
because everyone, my eight year old says,

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.780
Daddy, everyone can see Twitter.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:23.410
So unlike Facebook where actually,

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.933
you are being watched because
there's gonna be people,

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:29.880
like, in the middle of that firestorm,

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:33.850
I had someone contact me
from a security agency,

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:36.560
and they said take your
Facebook page down immediately,

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.900
and I said why, he said right now,

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:41.920
every comment and post
for the last nine years

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:44.890
is being screenshot for
future articles about you.

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.697
And so, I was, oh, that's
an excellent reason.

00:16:47.697 --> 00:16:48.530
(audience laughs)

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:50.610
So Twitter gives me an opportunity

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:55.480
to do my antifascist work but
without revealing where I am,

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:59.557
without revealing any
person details of my life,

00:16:59.557 --> 00:17:02.007
and I don't have any social
media that does that.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.460
- So since Twitter is visible by everyone,

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:09.070
how do you feel about interacting
with the general public?

00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:11.610
Do you, and what's your approach
to them, do you feel like

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:13.590
you're using it as a
teaching tool generally?

00:17:13.590 --> 00:17:16.423
Joanne, you mentioned that, and Teresa,

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:22.340
you know, I've seen everyone,
almost everyone here

00:17:22.340 --> 00:17:24.960
interact with friends, colleagues,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.300
people they know kind of personally,

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:28.690
how do you code-switch from speaking

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:33.283
to just a small group
of people to everyone?

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:37.840
- Well, there's two flavors of Twitter.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:39.070
I mean, it's still just as public,

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:41.230
but I think one of the ways
in which you can communicate

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:42.970
the message that you send when you use it

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:44.760
is I'm not being as public, right?

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.980
So you can send something
out to the entire world

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.220
or you can just respond to one person.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.500
Now, everyone can look at the thing

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:53.570
you send to that one person,

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:57.510
but it's not beamed out
automatically everywhere.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.723
So that's a little bit of code-switching.

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:05.470
I think it's really easy to forget

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.630
and underestimate the degree to which,

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:12.630
like your kid said, people
really see everything.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:17.813
And there have been moments, this morning.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:23.980
This morning, George Conway,
Kellyanne Conway's husband,

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:27.560
said something about the ABC interview

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.670
yesterday with the
president in which he said

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:33.420
he would take information
and maybe go to the FBI.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:37.990
And Conway said, the
president just said he might

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.430
take information, foreign information.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:45.430
And I said, no, it's not a
maybe, which he then retweeted.

00:18:46.577 --> 00:18:48.161
And I don't know what's
happening right now on Twitter.

00:18:48.161 --> 00:18:49.080
(audience laughs)

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:50.060
- [Teresa] Let's check.

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:51.250
- I know.

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:55.100
Anyway, so I just replied to him,

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.730
but he retweeted it and
now I don't, stuff's--

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:59.770
- I mean, I think the problem
with platforms like this

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:02.340
is you can't code-switch, there's nothing,

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:05.120
you have one message,
it goes to everybody,

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:07.130
and you have to think
very carefully about how,

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:10.280
I spend a long time
composing my Twitter messages

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:12.950
because they have to be
read, the tone of voice

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:17.020
has to be exactly right, read
by anybody from any background

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.730
with any language in any mood--

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:20.461
- For accuracy, right?
- At any time of day.

00:19:20.461 --> 00:19:21.294
- 'Cause we're academic.

00:19:21.294 --> 00:19:23.540
- Because it's gonna be
quoted back to me one day,

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:27.010
so I have deleted, I have
composed and not sent

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:30.020
many Twitter messages, it's just not safe.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:33.710
So it's, my own personal conversation

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:35.510
is way more lively than my Twitter feed,

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:38.660
but you can't do that,
that's really not wise.

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:40.100
- If you think about what George--

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:42.050
some of the academics
who've been taken down

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:43.903
by the right for their tweets,

00:19:44.758 --> 00:19:47.760
they're clearly, like take
George Ciccariello-Maher.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.827
His tweet was "All I wanted for--

00:19:50.827 --> 00:19:53.360
"All I want for Christmas
is white genocide."

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:57.390
It's clearly sarcastic,
it's clearly making a joke

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:59.040
that there's no white genocide.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:00.840
I mean, I talked to him after that,

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.323
he gets like 24/7 intense harassment,

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.970
the president of his
university denounced him

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:10.740
for a clearly sarcastic tweet.

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:14.360
So sarcasm doesn't, you
know, they will take it

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.530
completely out of context
and it's totally irrelevant

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:19.040
what you say afterwards.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:22.640
So you need to be very clear that like,

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.546
I try to be very earnest on Twitter.

00:20:25.546 --> 00:20:28.320
- And as an academic,
you also have to think

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.740
not just tone, but factually.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:34.370
And I guess this particularly
true for me as a historian.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:36.920
If I wanna say, you know, if you asked

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:39.920
the Founders this, they
probably would have said that,

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.130
again, that's within the
last day or two I did that,

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:43.800
and then I went back and I said,

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:45.050
I was gonna say they would have said,

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.280
and then I said no, many
of them would have--

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:48.970
- Probably would have.
- Right.

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:50.660
Because they all wouldn't have said it,

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:51.850
and somebody would come back at me

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.000
and then there'd be a conversation,

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.803
so you do have to think about every word,

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:58.500
you really have to think about your tone.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:02.130
I would say everyday,
I write and undo tweets

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:05.100
that make perfect sense to me
and I wish I could say them,

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.610
and then it's just not smart.

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:08.020
- [Fiona] Correct.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.860
- And notoriously, Twitter
does not have an edit button,

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.340
and that can be good in a way,

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.870
because people can't do things over,

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:17.540
they have to delete and rewrite.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.950
Michael, you've written a lot
about racial inequality gaps,

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.950
and what, do you get a
lot of online harassment,

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:29.470
do you get people who are attacking you?

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:34.470
- Yes, so I mean, I guess, the thing

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:37.690
that sort of motivates a
lot of what I write about

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:41.320
on Twitter and my tone is like,

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.250
so I guess maybe the strongest motivation

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.690
is to be truthful and to be factual,

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:47.270
so I spend a lot of
time just thinking like,

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:51.633
what's the truth here, and
that's what motivates what I say.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:58.110
I think that there's a little
bit of a difference in scale

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.330
to where, my harassment's not gonna rise

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:02.350
to the level of other people

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.273
because I just don't have
the magnitude of microphone.

00:22:07.275 --> 00:22:09.943
So it's not as intense.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:12.820
- [Emily] Have you blocked people?

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:16.644
- Sure, yeah, it's healthy to do that.

00:22:16.644 --> 00:22:19.190
(panelists laugh)
Yeah.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:20.500
So I mean, it's just something you monitor

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:22.710
and you're trying to be careful

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:24.380
but you're also trying to be truthful,

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.890
and I think that's, and
you're trying to be yourself

00:22:27.890 --> 00:22:30.560
in ways that it's allowed on the platform,

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:32.750
at least that's how I use it.

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.470
- But also, you know, you're tweeting

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:36.640
because you wanna communicate something,

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:39.000
and sometimes it's just to a colleague

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.760
or getting out information, but sometimes,

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:43.750
it's because people need to understand

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.403
checks and balances,
right, I mean, to teach.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:49.774
And again, you don't know how
many people are reading it,

00:22:49.774 --> 00:22:53.293
(air conditioner comes on)
but wow, that's dramatic,

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:57.700
but again, I guess I talked
about it as a teaching tool.

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.820
It's a remarkably powerful teaching tool.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.563
- [Fiona] Yeah, thousands of people.

00:23:01.563 --> 00:23:05.440
- Tens of thousands of people,
and I've been on a book tour

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.950
in the fall, that whole semester,

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:09.700
and everywhere I went
where people who knew me

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:13.640
from Twitter, commented about
things I'd said on Twitter,

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:18.130
young women who liked seeing
a woman say things in public.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:20.650
There were all kinds of ways
in which it just didn't dawn

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:25.650
on me, the impact of
what you do on Twitter.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:26.833
- That's interesting.

00:23:26.833 --> 00:23:28.800
So I sense that, do
you find it empowering?

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:32.120
Do you feel like you're
using your teaching voice,

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:34.740
your kind of nonprofit organizing voice,

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:36.590
both, your personal voice?

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:40.160
- I actually have a lot
of anxiety about tweeting.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:44.180
I think that, well, there
are two different ends

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:47.080
where I try to modify my voice.

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:49.440
One end in when I'm being too technical

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.650
and I get feedback from
people not in my field like,

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:53.930
we have no idea what you're saying,

00:23:53.930 --> 00:23:55.570
you're talking to like 10 people.

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.100
So I try to explain more

00:23:57.100 --> 00:23:59.870
and use more outward-facing language.

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:01.340
The other end is when there's something

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:03.810
that I really care about
and a lot of people

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.520
also really care about it,
and it's such a contentious,

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:10.380
divisive topic and there's
a huge social injustice,

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.560
and on the one hand, you
want to say something,

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:14.790
and on the other hand, you're just like,

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:17.040
no, that's not what my platform is for,

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:20.400
you know, people are
already commenting on this

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.270
and I don't wanna get sucked into that,

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.540
and what if what I say is misinterpreted,

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.910
so yeah, I'm really
cautious about tweeting,

00:24:27.910 --> 00:24:29.950
especially from the part
of the world that I'm from,

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:32.360
there's just so many (laughs), yeah.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.570
- Can you say more about that?

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:38.110
- I mean for example,
there was one professor

00:24:38.110 --> 00:24:41.080
that I heard about who
commented on comments,

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.600
on events happening in the Middle East

00:24:44.600 --> 00:24:47.870
and got fired, basically, because it was--

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:51.160
- The Middle East is--
- He was too emotional,

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:53.660
and so people interpreted
as like hate speech,

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:54.493
even though he felt he was--

00:24:54.493 --> 00:24:57.262
- Steven Salaita.
- Yeah, maybe.

00:24:57.262 --> 00:24:58.140
- [Jason] At the University
of Illinois or wherever,

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.950
who now drives a bus.

00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:02.280
- Right, a colleague of mine--
- A school bus.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:04.240
- From SOM Forward News article,

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.980
and I think he just
felt that he was talking

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:08.410
about an injustice and you know,

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:10.740
he felt really passionately
that this was wrong,

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:13.910
and then it got interpreted as
hate speech and he got fired.

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:17.770
So there's a fine balance
between speaking up

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:21.453
and not being heard in
the most productive way.

00:25:23.825 --> 00:25:27.290
- There's a way to
communicate, so recent--

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:30.820
without being, I've been working on trying

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:32.840
to communicate information without it

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:35.270
being possible for it to be an article

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:37.370
in the Washington Times or Breitbart.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:40.050
So for instance, Jared
Kushner recently had

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:42.570
this interview with Axios, and I noticed

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:44.540
that he was just parroting lines

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:48.550
from Frank Luntz's 2009
Israel Project report.

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:52.580
So what I did was I tweeted

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:56.940
the Frank Luntz's Israel Project report

00:25:56.940 --> 00:26:00.240
and a quote from it that Kushner said

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.370
and then it linked it to Kushner's,

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.073
so then nobody could take that

00:26:04.073 --> 00:26:07.360
it just said look, he
just tweeted a 100-page

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:11.695
document and quoted this part,
well that sounds familiar.

00:26:11.695 --> 00:26:15.400
So there are ways to
communicate information

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:18.080
without it being a headline.

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:23.080
'Cause some, like Salaita, the
tweet that he got fired for,

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:25.810
they couldn't fire, he
had moved from a non--

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:28.460
from a tenure track,
from a tenured position

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:31.450
to another position, but
the deans hadn't yet--

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.360
- They were trying--
- Approved his tenure.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:37.817
And so he tweeted "Netanyahu looks like

00:26:37.817 --> 00:26:41.047
"he's wearing teeth of
Palestinian children,"

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:45.923
but Salaita is Palestinian, so you know.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.850
And so yeah, so it's very important,

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:53.980
like any short line can be, and yeah.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:56.070
- [Joanne] Allowing other people
to say what you wanna say.

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:58.020
- Allowing other people
to say what you wanna say.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.640
- That's a very useful tip.
- That's very useful.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.120
- That's a great point.
- Yeah.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:04.144
- And it has a kind of academic
citation quality as well.

00:27:04.144 --> 00:27:05.102
- [Jason] Exactly.

00:27:05.102 --> 00:27:08.380
- And you take just link
to it and just retweet it.

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:10.960
- Well, importantly, you frame it.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:15.400
Here is someone who works
for Qualcomm saying this.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:17.670
Here is somebody who's
supposed to be enforcing

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.890
the antitrust laws to protect consumers,

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.870
and they're saying this.

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:24.200
Right, and neither part
of that is controversial.

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.390
It's factual what their job is,

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:28.410
and it's factual what they said,

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:31.780
but most of the people, the
person who wrote the article

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:35.920
you're linking to is not
framing the way I'm framing it,

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:40.920
and so it makes, the reader then sees it

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:44.530
in a different way and realizes
oh, there's a problem here.

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.120
- And the same thing with saying,

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:49.420
yesterday I think I said,
here's the emoluments clause

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:52.450
of the Constitution, here's a
link to the National Archives

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:56.000
so you can see the
Constitution, that's all I said.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:57.870
This is what it means, right?

00:27:57.870 --> 00:27:58.920
So I didn't say anything.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:00.250
- [Teresa] So you're planting the seed.

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:01.931
- Right, it's like
you're kind of wondering

00:28:01.931 --> 00:28:03.520
what this is and why people
are talking about it,

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:05.997
hey, here's the actual text.
- Right.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:12.170
- So with you know, there are
authoritarian governments,

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:16.439
there are actual censors around the world,

00:28:16.439 --> 00:28:19.900
then there's kind of one's own self-censor

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:21.800
who some of you have referred to,

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.878
and we have a variety of
states of tenure in our panel.

00:28:24.878 --> 00:28:26.520
(Jason laughs)

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:28.060
From any of your various institutions,

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:31.060
have you felt pressure
to tweet or not tweet

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:36.060
or to be a little bit more
judicious about what you say?

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:39.990
- I mean, alright, so I'll just say

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:43.950
I think Yale is a really
famous university,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.800
and anybody who has a job here

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:49.940
is sufficiently good
that their own reputation

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:52.420
is something that they care a lot about

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.130
and can do lots of things with,

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:57.640
whether at Yale or another
place anywhere around the world.

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:02.050
So for me, it's not about
what the university wants,

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:05.340
it's about what my mission is,

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:07.000
which I believe is totally consistent

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:07.960
with the university's mission,

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:10.250
but I'm not thinking about
it as a Yale problem,

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:12.910
I'm thinking about it
as a making the world

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:14.690
a better place problem and allowing me

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.090
to be effective in doing
that for as long as possible,

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:19.480
which means I need to stay relevant

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:23.110
and truthful and direct and
all those other good things.

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:25.810
So I don't think there's a conflict,

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:28.830
and I think maybe if you were
asked at a different place,

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:29.900
you would get a different answer,

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:34.210
but I think, I mean everybody
here is super eminent

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:37.740
and is probably thinking the same way,

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:39.012
but I'll let you all
answer for yourselves.

00:29:39.012 --> 00:29:40.683
- Well no, the question, you know,

00:29:40.683 --> 00:29:42.330
that I ask myself with every tweet

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:44.710
is like, how is this gonna hurt me, right?

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:46.500
Is this gonna really smack me in the face?

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:49.140
That's equivalent to, what does it mean

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.600
that I'm a Yale professor tweeting this?

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:55.190
So it's, you can't particularly
at an institution like Yale,

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:56.680
you can't separate your identity

00:29:56.680 --> 00:29:58.580
from the fact that that's who you are.

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:06.550
- Well, I never really
thought about it in that way

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:09.390
because I mostly, I like to
use Twitter as a connector.

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.620
This is just one of my hobbies
and my passions in life,

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:14.670
I love to connect people,
and so when I tweet,

00:30:14.670 --> 00:30:17.790
it's mostly tweeting work
that other people have done

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:20.370
or work that I've done,
like check out this paper,

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:22.100
or oh, great job on this paper,

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.600
or look what this organization
has just achieved.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.170
So it's nothing really that I feel

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:28.780
I would be held accountable for

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.137
or that I'm representing
Yale or anything like that.

00:30:31.137 --> 00:30:33.290
I think that a university like Yale

00:30:33.290 --> 00:30:35.250
gives you a platform to better

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:36.980
and more strongly connect people,

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:40.660
and Twitter is one channel
through which you can do that.

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:42.940
- Yeah, so I just, one of the ways

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.540
that you can think about your relationship

00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:47.030
to your employer on Twitter is like,

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:48.620
well I better watch what I say,

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:51.550
but another one is the
reach of your scholarship

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:54.300
is much greater to the extent
that you're engaging with it.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:58.390
So you know, papers that
I haven't tweeted about,

00:30:58.390 --> 00:31:02.070
my mom has read them, and
then papers that I have,

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:03.810
you know, thousands of people will engage

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:08.350
in some form with it, be it
like a pull quote from it

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:11.910
or graphs from it, right, so,
I think it goes both ways.

00:31:11.910 --> 00:31:14.610
You have to think about
what this looks like

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:16.310
as a professor at this institution,

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.210
but it's also, you're communicating,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:19.650
connecting to your field in ways

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:22.660
that you know, gets you invited to talks

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:24.930
because people heard about your work

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:27.350
because they saw you
write about it on Twitter,

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:31.660
and these are people who you
know, again, in our field,

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:34.520
a lot of its reputation based,
they're gonna be writing,

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:35.840
if we're gonna get really career about it,

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:37.580
they're gonna be writing
tenure letters and the like,

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.730
so as someone untenured,
I think it cuts both ways

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.073
and it's important to
think about that too.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.550
- I prefer Twitter because on Facebook

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:50.740
I did embarrass Yale a little
bit by, there were, so,

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:52.880
the incident that I got involved with was

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:55.320
a philosophy professor David Koch

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:58.240
arguing that homosexuality
is a disability,

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:01.152
and I used some potty language
in describing the talk.

00:32:01.152 --> 00:32:02.050
(audience laughs)

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:05.309
So, some potty words, so
no potty words on Twitter.

00:32:05.309 --> 00:32:06.600
(panelists laugh)

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:09.460
But generally, I think my, you know,

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:11.340
I have the luxury of being tenured,

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.930
but I've tried just to
speak my mind my whole life,

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:15.670
and if Yale's gonna fire me for that,

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:17.860
Yale's gonna fire me for that.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.840
- So those are some of the
stickier moments of Twitter.

00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:22.710
What's the most fun for you?

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:25.450
Where do you, I know, you know,

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:28.940
Michael, I know you do
tweet about your family,

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:32.560
about sports and other fun things.

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:34.900
Is that, does that take
you down a rabbit hole

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:36.241
or is that a good rabbit hole?

00:32:36.241 --> 00:32:38.639
- (laughs) No, I mean, all of it's fine,

00:32:38.639 --> 00:32:40.379
everything's fine, everything's fine.

00:32:40.379 --> 00:32:44.020
(panelists laugh)
- He said nervously.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:46.880
- No, what I would say is that

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:50.070
when I think about what
I'm like on Twitter,

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:52.760
I think about who's watching,
and a lot of the people

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:57.100
who watch and follow and
engage with me on Twitter

00:32:57.100 --> 00:33:00.600
are graduate students, right,
they're undergraduates,

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.740
they're recent graduates of our program,

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:06.790
our MBA program, and so if I can be my,

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:10.550
more of my full self in public,

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:15.110
it invites more and
different kinds of people

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:19.070
to also be themselves in academic spaces.

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:20.270
One of the things I really care about

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.726
is having a more equitable university,

00:33:23.726 --> 00:33:25.790
and I think it's a value that Yale shares.

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:29.180
We don't always uphold that value,

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:32.040
and so one of the barriers
to it is that you know,

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.680
academics kind of all tend
to look a certain way,

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:36.810
but if you allow for more difference,

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:38.750
you communicate that normatively,

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:41.530
you can actually create changes and norms,

00:33:41.530 --> 00:33:44.580
so I think that that
too, so the two months

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:46.900
during the NBA playoffs
when my Twitter feed

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:50.960
is just Warriors gifs,
(Jason laughs)

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:54.380
part of that is because I
really like basketball a lot,

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:58.410
but also, a part of
it's being a full person

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:02.290
in a visible space that allows
other people to see that.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:04.017
- I have a goofy example of that.

00:34:04.017 --> 00:34:04.850
- Please.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.510
- Trivial yet funny.

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:08.220
So there's a, and it's actually an example

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:12.410
of things that Twitter can
do that you don't imagine.

00:34:12.410 --> 00:34:15.250
So there's a bunch of historians who,

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:16.920
we used to do it Sunday
night, and now I think

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:18.710
it's Saturday night or
moving back to Sunday night,

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:23.070
but at nine o'clock at night,
this community of historians

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:25.970
goes on Twitter and watches
a historical movie together

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:28.470
and live-tweets a response
to what we're seeing.

00:34:28.470 --> 00:34:29.710
It's actually, if you're interested,

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:33.740
it's hashtag H-A-T-M,
Historians at the Movies.

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:36.620
So very early on, and now
the public is in there too

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:38.020
and it's become much bigger.

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:40.630
So very early on in the process,

00:34:40.630 --> 00:34:43.773
I somehow mentioned that I'd
never seen National Treasure,

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:47.010
so then that became
the movie we looked at,

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:49.340
and I was screaming in outrage,

00:34:49.340 --> 00:34:52.310
you know, and they put lemon juice

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:54.380
on the Declaration of Independence, right?

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:55.213
(audience laughs)

00:34:55.213 --> 00:34:57.130
But the writers of National Treasure

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:59.180
saw this and beamed in.
- Oh cool.

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:01.330
- So then it was
historians commenting on it

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:04.360
and the writers commenting on it,

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:07.720
and I literally did
say, you put lemon juice

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:09.330
on the Declaration of Independence!

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:11.870
(panelists laugh)
And the writers were like,

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:12.970
we didn't realize it would seem

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:15.880
like that big a deal
to historians (laughs).

00:35:15.880 --> 00:35:19.530
So that was just fun, but
that's such a Twitter moment,

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:23.360
when suddenly you're like wait,
I'm talking to the writers?

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.100
And they're listening to historians?

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:26.550
Like how interesting is that?

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:29.460
- So I only use Twitter for work,

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:30.880
but there's different kinds of work,

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:32.470
and I think the most fun I had

00:35:32.470 --> 00:35:34.930
was when we were running
a Kickstarter campaign

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:36.780
with my organization to support

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:39.610
a bunch of refugee women that
we're trying to crowdfund

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:43.720
for a food truck to help
scale their catering company.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:46.640
So it's a really random pitch,

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:49.960
and so far, we had only
had about a hundred

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:53.380
high net worth individuals
that were in our close circle

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:56.020
financing all these
ventures, and when we did

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:59.280
the Kickstarter campaign,
we got 800 individuals

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:00.870
from around the world from countries

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.270
that these women had never heard of

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:03.770
and that they would have never dreamed

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:06.490
that these people had heard of them,

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:08.670
and so Twitter was a huge part of that

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:10.990
because we were really systematic

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:12.660
about when to tweet and who to tweet to,

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:14.370
and we got Susan Sarandon to tweet it,

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:16.650
and you know, celebrities
and random things like that,

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:20.350
so it was so much fun,
and it was also exhausting

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.670
'cause it lasted for two months

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:25.360
and you worry that people get
fatigued from your campaign.

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:27.840
But we had an awesome
time and it got funded,

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:30.440
and it was just the most
incredible experience ever.

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:35.060
- Joanne, I know you've
talked about engaging

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:39.430
with people with, especially
with Founding Fathers fans,

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:42.130
you had a real, grab bag of people.

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:43.021
- Yes (laughs).

00:36:43.021 --> 00:36:44.280
- Am I characterizing that--

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:46.357
- There is a grab bag of people, yes.

00:36:46.357 --> 00:36:48.900
- And so all of you I imagine
have engaged with people who,

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:50.320
I mean, there's a teaching tool

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:54.930
for people like your
students and people who,

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:57.670
you know, are fact based and
so forth, and then there are

00:36:57.670 --> 00:37:00.330
people who are violently
opposed to your ideas,

00:37:00.330 --> 00:37:05.330
what is it, Jason for instance,

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:07.390
what is that like when
people, when you're trying,

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:09.983
do engage and try to teach
people who don't agree with you?

00:37:09.983 --> 00:37:13.320
- I don't engage generally
with the people who,

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:16.680
I get a lot of sort of
international tweets,

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:20.250
I mean, tweets in Portuguese
'cause I'm doing stuff

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:24.320
in different countries,
but, that are to be--

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.703
I mean, Brazil seems to have sort of,

00:37:26.703 --> 00:37:30.540
from what I recognized,
somewhat violent culture

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:32.430
directed among their far right.

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:37.430
But I always engage extremely
civilly, I always just,

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:41.860
if I engage, it's always respectfully

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:43.940
and civilly on Twitter.

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:45.810
I mean Facebook I think was dangerous

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:48.890
because it gave you this
illusion of privacy,

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:50.930
and Twitter, I just try to model,

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:53.890
unfortunately I come from a
very combative profession,

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:56.270
analytic philosophy, where the only form

00:37:56.270 --> 00:37:59.383
of a question is you're
wrong because dot, dot, dot.

00:37:59.383 --> 00:38:02.510
(panelists laugh)
So we're all used to,

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:04.070
and we're all used to odd views,

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:05.400
like I had a former colleague,

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:06.950
I have a colleague here at Yale

00:38:06.950 --> 00:38:08.580
who believes there's only one thing,

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:11.998
so you know, I'm used to strange views--

00:38:11.998 --> 00:38:13.231
- Economists are like that too.

00:38:13.231 --> 00:38:14.602
- I'm used to combative discourse.

00:38:14.602 --> 00:38:19.120
So I only engage civilly and respectfully,

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:21.350
even with, you know, neo-Nazis.

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.590
- So I would say that one big decision

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:27.520
that you make when you're on Twitter

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:31.780
is which people to respond to.
- Right.

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:34.840
- So there are plenty of
people who disagree with me

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:36.830
and write something that
I don't even understand,

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:39.330
or they write something
that's totally ignorant

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:41.580
and uninformed, and you think,

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:44.670
I could teach them, but
they're not paying tuition,

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:47.450
so that would be a long, complex process,

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:50.940
I probably wouldn't succeed,
and it would take a lot of time

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:53.050
and I don't even know who they are.

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.770
So the people that I tend to respond to

00:38:55.770 --> 00:38:58.080
and try to teach are the
ones who have a name,

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:00.790
somebody who is actually
another faculty member

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:03.720
at another university or is a reporter

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:04.627
of some stature or whatever--

00:39:04.627 --> 00:39:06.910
- And a picture usually too, right?

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:10.180
- So it's not anonymous, they're
in it with their reputation

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:13.230
as well, and then, and
I'm doing the civil thing,

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:17.540
explaining, here's why,
but I think if you try

00:39:17.540 --> 00:39:19.730
to respond to people, there's a danger

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:22.330
that you go down into the
mud pit if you respond

00:39:22.330 --> 00:39:27.330
to people who are not
operating at that same level.

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:32.520
And so I try to avoid
responding to people,

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:36.130
sort of will make a very
provocative tweet in response

00:39:36.130 --> 00:39:38.120
and obviously trying to get me to engage,

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:41.500
and I feel like engagement
is itself an endorsement,

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:45.250
and I am not going to
endorse people who are not

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:48.700
on the debate in the same terms that I am.

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:51.020
- Yeah, the screenshot, the provocative,

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:54.630
the trying to get you to respond angrily

00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:57.670
and get a screenshot of that,
I've had like fake articles

00:39:57.670 --> 00:40:01.650
done of like, provocative
responses that I did.

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:04.010
You know, people provoking me
and then me saying something

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:06.300
and then they'll put a
different context around it.

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:10.350
So you have to be aware when
you're on those platforms

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:12.339
what people try to do to get you

00:40:12.339 --> 00:40:15.157
to respond in an uncivil way.

00:40:15.157 --> 00:40:17.910
- But you know occasionally,
and I do exactly what you do,

00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:22.370
which is, if someone insults
me, I'm not gonna respond.

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:26.290
If someone is screaming or doesn't,

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:27.123
there's a whole category
of people like that

00:40:27.123 --> 00:40:29.100
that I would not respond to.

00:40:29.100 --> 00:40:32.630
But I have had people contact me

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:34.130
who are angry about something I've said,

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:37.790
aren't insulting me,
aren't venting anger at me

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:40.700
but clearly think differently than I do

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:43.683
and I clearly think, well
if you knew this thing--

00:40:43.683 --> 00:40:45.280
- Right, it would help a lot.
- You might think differently.

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:47.080
And I have responded to those people,

00:40:47.080 --> 00:40:50.350
and I have to confess, it
happened more two years ago

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:53.120
than it happens now, but more than once,

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:53.953
someone would say, well you don't know

00:40:53.953 --> 00:40:56.220
what you're talking about
because blah, blah, blah,

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:58.670
and I would say well, and I would erase

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:02.930
all emotion from my response,
and I would say well--

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:04.110
- Have you read this?

00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:05.170
- It's interesting that you say that,

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:07.190
but I actually think this and this is why.

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:09.730
And more than once, I had
someone who clearly didn't agree

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:11.870
with me politically respond by going,

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:13.880
huh, I'm gonna think about that.

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:15.360
And you know, it's one person--

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:16.193
- Makes you feel good.
- But right,

00:41:16.193 --> 00:41:18.840
it's like there was a moment
of communication there

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:22.480
that you know, at least
in my understanding

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:24.310
of the current ethos, it's nice to know

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:25.480
that you can have that moment.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:28.150
And again, it reconfirms the fact

00:41:28.150 --> 00:41:31.330
that the teaching potential of Twitter

00:41:31.330 --> 00:41:34.860
used in a savvy kind of a way is powerful.

00:41:34.860 --> 00:41:35.820
- And also, you don't win

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:38.070
an argument by showing great emotion.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:40.670
You win an argument by remaining calm.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:43.790
Like, when I was a junior faculty member,

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:46.870
someone said, you know, you
celebrate in the end zone

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:49.390
before you've won the
game too much in talks,

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:50.831
when I see that I'm gonna win.

00:41:50.831 --> 00:41:52.510
(panelists laugh)

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:55.323
Celebrating in the end zone
takes away from your point.

00:41:56.710 --> 00:41:58.850
- It's interesting because I know there's,

00:41:58.850 --> 00:42:00.320
I've seen an argument a lot on Twitter

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:03.050
that especially women, people of color,

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:06.090
and other marginalized
groups are not responsible

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:07.980
for teaching or they should be compensated

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:10.740
in some way for teaching,
but you're all teachers.

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:12.700
So how does that, does it sort of,

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:15.300
does that trump the sort of sense of like,

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:19.790
well, I'm not here for your education.

00:42:19.790 --> 00:42:21.470
But it sounds like kind of are.

00:42:21.470 --> 00:42:22.600
- No, of course we are.
- Yeah.

00:42:22.600 --> 00:42:25.133
- We're teachers, that gets the mission.

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:27.670
And if we can reach, you know,

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:30.240
the number of people I can
teach in the classroom is small,

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.020
but if I can teach thousands of people

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:34.490
on the internet, that's fantastic.

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:38.853
- And current students follow,
former students follow,

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:42.120
so that's also, it's continuing,

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:46.010
and you can see them
engaging further with ideas,

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:48.910
so yeah, I think, I mean,
that's the main reason

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:50.710
I think I'm there.

00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:51.543
- I agree.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:53.807
- Michael, you would agree with that?

00:42:53.807 --> 00:42:55.560
- Oh, absolutely.

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:58.424
Yeah, with everything
that's said, yeah (laughs).

00:42:58.424 --> 00:43:00.620
- So I know we're running,
maybe you have time

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:02.720
for one or two questions?

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:04.182
Yes.

00:43:04.182 --> 00:43:05.240
- [Questioner 1] Relatively recently,

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:07.910
a transgender woman in academic philosophy

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:09.816
wrote about her experience
of being driven out

00:43:09.816 --> 00:43:11.731
of the field because of discrimination.

00:43:11.731 --> 00:43:14.791
She posted on Twitter, she said what,

00:43:14.791 --> 00:43:19.430
basically, existing online is precarious,

00:43:19.430 --> 00:43:21.880
but that precarity is not
distributed equitably.

00:43:22.874 --> 00:43:27.791
The second professional
interactions take place on Twitter,

00:43:28.927 --> 00:43:32.240
it creates sort of a boys club.

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:34.920
- So this is my field,
my field is notorious

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:38.840
for its viciousness, for
its patriarchal sentiment,

00:43:38.840 --> 00:43:42.130
for its, and many of the senior men

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:44.770
in my profession have decided
suddenly they're feminists

00:43:44.770 --> 00:43:48.630
because now there's this movement of,

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:50.730
I wanna be careful about what I say,

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:55.730
but now there's this movement
of gender critical feminists

00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:00.880
who are argue that transgender
women aren't really women,

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:02.780
and so suddenly, there's all these people

00:44:02.780 --> 00:44:05.850
who I remember as having hated feminism,

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:07.270
suddenly they're like, I'm a feminist,

00:44:07.270 --> 00:44:09.220
because I'm, you know.

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:12.580
So yeah, so there's very,
academic philosophy has often had

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:17.030
a very vicious, part of
it is our general culture,

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:20.253
and we're, you know,

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:25.643
I've tangled with the
forces you've mentioned.

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:29.880
And it, you know, you can't
really call it a boys club

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:32.590
because you have that
U.K. group of feminists,

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:33.930
and you know, they are feminists

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:38.930
and I respect their views, but
they're definitely championed

00:44:39.160 --> 00:44:44.160
by the senior men in philosophy,

00:44:44.570 --> 00:44:47.593
and so it's been a bad situation.

00:44:49.604 --> 00:44:53.810
It's so intense that, I
mean, I tweet about trans--

00:44:53.810 --> 00:44:55.750
I mean I write about transgender issues,

00:44:55.750 --> 00:44:56.870
I've written about it in my book,

00:44:56.870 --> 00:45:01.350
because all over the world,
these far-right movements,

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:04.700
neo-fascist movements
attack transgender women.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:07.450
You know, turning boys
into girls, is like,

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:10.060
so I'm like, well, I'm fine for people

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:11.500
to have discussions about gender,

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:13.960
but how do you negotiate this fact?

00:45:13.960 --> 00:45:16.690
- I think you're asking a
really interesting question,

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:20.240
which is that, is this a platform

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:23.280
to reduce inequalities and disparities,

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:25.880
or does it actually promote and exacerbate

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:27.610
existing inequalities and disparities?

00:45:27.610 --> 00:45:29.570
And I'd love to hear what others think.

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:32.530
- So for economics, it reduces them.

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:35.395
I mean, I can't be interrupted
on Twitter, for example.

00:45:35.395 --> 00:45:37.090
(panelists laugh)
I just, I tweet.

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:38.790
So I would say there's no boys club,

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:40.960
I don't perceive any kind of boys club,

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:45.710
I don't perceive any kind of
issue in the economics debate.

00:45:45.710 --> 00:45:48.403
- But I do, I do think
related to your question,

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:52.010
people with privilege of various sorts

00:45:52.010 --> 00:45:54.400
have louder voices
because they can, right?

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:56.240
And they're not as vulnerable.

00:45:56.240 --> 00:45:57.940
And Twitter does do that.

00:45:57.940 --> 00:46:01.260
But you know, what that
then means to me is,

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:05.363
okay, I'm a tenured
professor, I teach at Yale,

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:08.787
I can get out there and
say something, right,

00:46:08.787 --> 00:46:11.920
I can say something that
I feel needs to be said

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:15.620
and I have that privilege
and I can use that privilege.

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:19.690
So I wouldn't say it's a boys club,

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:23.410
but I feel it's important
to have a loud female voice.

00:46:23.410 --> 00:46:27.420
And you know, the fact that other women,

00:46:27.420 --> 00:46:29.330
women graduate students,
high school girls,

00:46:29.330 --> 00:46:32.180
have responded that way
made me think about that

00:46:32.180 --> 00:46:33.473
in a way that I hadn't thought about it.

00:46:33.473 --> 00:46:35.930
- Well in philosophy I should say,

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:38.720
there are very vocal women feminists

00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:43.720
attacking transgender women,
so it's a very bad situation,

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:48.820
and I do not weigh in on
it, because whenever a male

00:46:48.820 --> 00:46:52.950
weighs in on it, they're
accused of being a misogynist.

00:46:52.950 --> 00:46:55.750
So if I would say, I stand up,

00:46:55.750 --> 00:46:58.140
so it's a very particular situation,

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:01.730
and you know, 100% of the--

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:04.650
yeah, it's, but it has to
do with this privilege,

00:47:04.650 --> 00:47:07.440
you know, it's a common reaction.

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:08.750
- I think one more quick question

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:09.810
and then we have to wrap up.

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:11.739
Please, yes.

00:47:11.739 --> 00:47:12.932
- [Questioner 2] So Twitter tends to form

00:47:12.932 --> 00:47:15.140
these micro communities,
like if you were looking

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:17.973
as an economist, you might
be in the economy Twitter,

00:47:17.973 --> 00:47:19.342
there's philosophy Twitter.

00:47:19.342 --> 00:47:20.210
But after making an effort,

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:21.550
especially after you get used to it,

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:25.830
engage with people outside
of your specific discipline.

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:29.009
Like I feel that that's
Twitter's wonderful opportunity

00:47:29.009 --> 00:47:30.060
platform for--

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:33.540
- So I'll go first 'cause
I'm short, I don't,

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:35.020
that's not why I'm on Twitter.

00:47:35.020 --> 00:47:36.930
I'm on Twitter to talk about antitrust

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:40.100
and healthcare policy to people
who are interested in that,

00:47:40.100 --> 00:47:41.280
and if they're interested in me,

00:47:41.280 --> 00:47:43.983
that's fine, but I'm not
trying to reach out to anybody.

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:46.470
- I guess I'll be the opposite,

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:47.730
'cause that's the whole point that,

00:47:47.730 --> 00:47:49.280
that's the whole reason
that I'm on Twitter,

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:51.040
is to engage with, well I wanna engage

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:52.130
with people within public health

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:53.500
and also outside public health.

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:54.820
Like now I'm in a management school,

00:47:54.820 --> 00:47:56.990
and I wanna get people who so far

00:47:56.990 --> 00:47:58.990
are not interested in
social entrepreneurship

00:47:58.990 --> 00:48:01.940
or social impact broadly speaking
to be interested in that.

00:48:01.940 --> 00:48:04.403
And you have to use certain hashtags,

00:48:05.330 --> 00:48:08.416
but yeah, I think that it's true,

00:48:08.416 --> 00:48:12.140
you can go down into a vortex
and get even more specific,

00:48:12.140 --> 00:48:14.750
or you can really create
a snowball effect,

00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:15.900
it could go either way.

00:48:17.586 --> 00:48:20.650
- I avoid, I mean, a lot of my life

00:48:20.650 --> 00:48:25.650
has been trying to avoid
the ugliness of my field,

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:28.040
and I'm very happy to be involved

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:30.110
in a larger political discourse

00:48:30.110 --> 00:48:33.920
outside of the gossip about my profession.

00:48:33.920 --> 00:48:38.220
- Whereas for me, a big part of my sense

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:41.490
of being a historian is public history,

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:44.280
'cause a historian should
be talking to the public.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:47.680
I give public lectures,
I co-host a podcast,

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:50.440
I do this in a lot of ways,
and what I now understand

00:48:50.440 --> 00:48:53.260
is that Twitter is another way to do that,

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:54.690
and a powerful one.

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:56.640
Might not be recognized that way yet,

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:00.840
but I think, to me, kind of
like what Teresa was saying,

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:01.750
that's the point of it,

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:04.130
is to not just talk to my colleagues,

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:05.170
although I love talking to them,

00:49:05.170 --> 00:49:07.807
but it really is to reach out.

00:49:07.807 --> 00:49:10.530
- It's hard to do philosophy,
a philosophical argument,

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:13.200
in 280 characters.
(panelists laugh)

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:15.273
You ever read the Critique of Pure Reason?

00:49:16.420 --> 00:49:19.100
- Oh well, Michael, do you
want the last word on that?

00:49:19.100 --> 00:49:20.326
- No, I'm good.
- Okay.

00:49:20.326 --> 00:49:21.337
(panelists laugh)

00:49:21.337 --> 00:49:24.880
Well here is everyone
that you see before you.

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:26.160
I hope you'll follow everybody.

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:29.600
Everyone's immensely
edifying and entertaining,

00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:34.370
and it's a treat to be, to
know you all on Twitter,

00:49:34.370 --> 00:49:36.870
and in some cases, to know you in person,

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:38.204
so thank you very much.

00:49:38.204 --> 00:49:39.430
- Thank you Emily.
- Thank you Emily.

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:44.430
- Thank you.
(audience applauds)

00:49:45.992 --> 00:49:49.659
(gentle instrumental music)

